# Schema migrations

Alembic owns the database schema. SQLAlchemy models in `app/models/` are the
source of truth for the shape, and every change is captured as a reviewed
revision here.

## Commands

    # Apply everything (this is what `python -m app.cli migrate` runs)
    alembic upgrade head

    # After editing app/models/*.py, generate a revision and read it before committing
    alembic revision --autogenerate -m "add schedule catchup column"

    # Inspect / roll back
    alembic current
    alembic history --verbose
    alembic downgrade -1

    # Emit SQL without touching the database (useful for a DBA review)
    alembic upgrade head --sql

The target database comes from `DATABASE_URL` (see `.env.example`), so the same
commands work against SQLite locally and PostgreSQL in a deployment.

## Notes

* SQLite cannot alter most column definitions, so `env.py` enables Alembic's
  `render_as_batch` mode and rewrites tables instead.
* The initial revision creates the full schema, including the indexes that make
  task claiming and run history fast (`ix_step_claimable`,
  `ix_run_workflow_idempotency`, `ix_schedule_due`).
* `ix_run_workflow_idempotency` is unique: it is what makes schedule duplicate
  protection and run idempotency keys authoritative under concurrency.
* Timestamps are stored as timezone-naive UTC in every dialect, so SQLite and
  PostgreSQL behave identically. API responses re-attach UTC.
